    en: `
    <p><strong>General Natural Environment</strong></p>

    <p>Estonia is one of Europe's cleanest and most environmentally friendly countries. Despite its relatively small size, it offers remarkable natural diversity, including dense forests, thousands of lakes, vast wetlands, scenic coastlines, islands, limestone cliffs, and fertile plains. More than <strong>50% of the country's territory is covered by forests</strong>, while a significant proportion of its landscapes enjoys protected status.</p>

    <p>Thanks to its low population density and strong environmental policies, Estonia has preserved many untouched ecosystems. Numerous national parks, nature reserves, and protected areas safeguard the country's unique Baltic biodiversity and provide excellent opportunities for ecotourism.</p>

    <p><strong>Forests and Wetlands</strong></p>

    <p>Forests are Estonia's greatest natural treasure. Pine, spruce, birch, aspen, and alder dominate the landscape. During spring and summer the forests become lush and green, while autumn attracts thousands of people searching for mushrooms and wild berries. Sustainable forestry practices help ensure continuous regeneration of woodland ecosystems.</p>

    <p>Estonia is equally famous for its extensive <strong>bogs and wetlands</strong>, which cover nearly one-fifth of the country. They are among Europe's best-preserved peatland ecosystems. Wooden boardwalks allow visitors to safely explore these unique landscapes while protecting their fragile environment.</p>

    <p><strong>Lakes, Rivers, and Coastline</strong></p>

    <p>Estonia is home to more than <strong>1,200 lakes</strong>. The largest is <strong>Lake Peipus</strong>, shared with Russia, while Lake Võrtsjärv ranks second. Although the country's rivers are generally short, they play an important role in local ecosystems, freshwater resources, and fishing.</p>

    <p>Including its islands, Estonia's coastline stretches for nearly <strong>3,800 kilometers</strong>. The country has more than <strong>2,200 islands</strong>, with Saaremaa and Hiiumaa being the largest. The Baltic coast is famous for sandy beaches, rocky shores, picturesque bays, and historic lighthouses.</p>

    <p><strong>Wildlife</strong></p>

    <p>Estonia's forests provide habitat for <strong>brown bears, wolves, lynxes, moose, roe deer, wild boar, foxes, beavers, and otters</strong>. The country is regarded as one of Europe's finest destinations for observing large wild mammals. More than <strong>380 bird species</strong> have also been recorded, including white-tailed eagles, black storks, common cranes, and numerous migratory birds traveling along the Baltic flyway.</p>

    <p><strong>Climate</strong></p>

    <p>Estonia has a <strong>temperate maritime climate with continental influences</strong>. The Baltic Sea moderates temperatures, producing milder winters than many inland parts of Eastern Europe while keeping summers pleasantly warm.</p>

    <p>Average temperatures range from approximately <strong>–1 to –7 °C in January</strong> and <strong>+16 to +20 °C in July</strong>. Rainfall is distributed fairly evenly throughout the year, and snow usually covers much of the country during winter. Spring arrives gradually, summer features very long daylight hours, autumn brings colorful forests, and winter transforms the landscape into a snowy destination ideal for outdoor recreation.</p>
  `,
